WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.740
- There is no other way to put it.

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.080
Roe v. Wade and its progeny are dead.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.970
Women in the United States no longer have

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.580
a constitutional guaranteed
right to an abortion.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.100
This video explains the how and the why

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.780
of everything that happened.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.390
And to do that, we have
to start with the history

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.690
of how we got Roe v.
Wade in the first place.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.640
What is the source of
a constitutional right

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.410
to an abortion in the United States?

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.450
It is one of the rights which fall under

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.950
a general right to privacy,

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.260
which is the right of people
to make personal decisions

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.970
regarding intimate matters.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.430
Now, when you're talking
about the right to privacy,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.170
you're talking about unenumerated rights

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.430
in the constitution.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.560
When the founders were
drafting the constitution,

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.290
they did not set out to
list all of the rights

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.750
which Americans could conceivably claim.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.580
They were well aware that
by naming some of the rights

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.290
but not others, future governments

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.330
might deny the people rights

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:50.100
that the founders believed were important.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.280
And this is why the Federalists
originally did not want

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.670
a bill of rights to be included
in the constitution itself.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.640
And as a compromise, rather
than make a long list of rights,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.170
the founders included the Ninth Amendment,

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.960
which states, quote, "The
enumeration in the constitution,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.730
of certain rights, shall not be construed

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:09.030
to deny or disparage others
retained by the people."

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.310
James Madison in
particular reportedly said

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.050
that the government should
never use the bill of rights

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.910
as an excuse to increase its powers

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.130
over areas of life that
were not enumerated

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.300
in the constitution.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.820
Now, the Supreme Court
really began fleshing out

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.400
unenumerated rights in
the mid 20th century,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.280
particularly in a case called
Griswold v. Connecticut,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.140
which voided a statute
that made it illegal

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.210
for married people to buy contraception.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.280
The Court wrote that privacy
was one of the values

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.600
implied by the First Amendment,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.240
which protects the right of
association between people,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.380
by the Fourth Amendment,

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.660
which allows people to be
secure in their persons

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.740
from government intrusions

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.680
and through the 14th Amendment's
guarantee of due process.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.080
Now, in that case, after
reviewing a line of cases

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.490
in which the Supreme
Court identified rights

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.590
not explicitly enumerated
in the constitution,

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.270
Justice Douglas argued that the Court

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.530
could infer a right to privacy

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.060
by looking at zones of privacy

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.310
protected by other parts
of the constitution

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.410
in overlapping protections.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.050
Quote, "Various guarantees
create zones of privacy.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.970
The right of association
contained in the penumbra

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.980
of the First Amendment
is one, as we have seen.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.560
The Third Amendment in its
prohibition against quartering

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.590
of soldiers in any house in time of peace

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.760
without the consent of the owner

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.500
is another facet of that privacy.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.270
The Fourth Amendment explicitly affirms

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.220
the right of the people to
be secure in their persons,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.750
houses, papers, and effects,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.880
against unreasonable
searches and seizures.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.710
The Fifth Amendment in its
Self-Incrimination Clause

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.140
enables the citizen to
create a zone of privacy

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.270
which government may not
force him to surrender

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.200
to his detriment.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.880
The Ninth Amendment
provides the enumeration

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.230
in the Constitution, of certain rights,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.240
shall not be construed
to deny or disparage

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.680
others retained by the people."

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.350
Now, Griswold upheld the
right of married couples

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.480
to decide private issues of contraception.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.970
And seven years later, Eisenstadt v. Baird

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.730
extended Griswold's
holding to unmarried people

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.170
using some of the same logic.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.630
And following Griswold, the Court upheld

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.720
a right to privacy and
struck down a number of laws,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.330
including those prohibiting
interracial marriage.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:55.950
The constitution does not explicitly say

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:56.940
anything about marriage.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.860
And in the mid 19th century,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.990
the law offered interracial
couples no protection

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:02.550
even from prosecution.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.560
In Loving v. Virginia, the Court held

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.840
that a Virginia law criminalizing
interracial marriage

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.300
violated the due process
clause of the 14th Amendment.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.597
Chief Justice Earl Warren wrote,

00:03:10.597 --> 00:03:12.570
"Under our constitution
the freedom to marry

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.460
or not marry a person of another race

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.780
resides with the individual

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.490
and cannot be infringed by the state."

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.950
And continuing with that
logic, the Court also relied

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.780
on the 14th Amendment's due process laws

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.670
to strike down laws banning prisoners

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.230
from getting married while in prison.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:29.010
And in continuing in those
line of cases, in 1973,

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.230
Roe v. Wade established
a constitutional right

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.020
to an abortion through the
Fifth Amendment, among others.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:35.970
And the Fifth Amendment says
no person shall be deprived

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.790
of life, liberty, or property
without due process of law.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.310
Now, courts have interpreted
two types of due process.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.900
One is procedural due process,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.580
which are the rules that
the government must follow,

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.760
such as giving a person notice, a hearing,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.350
and a fair tribunal.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.360
And then there's what's called
the substantive due process,

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.070
which is the idea that allows courts

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:56.370
to establish and protect
certain fundamental rights

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:57.420
from government interference

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.460
even if procedural protections are present

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.440
or the rights are unenumerated.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.660
Now, the Roe opinion
begins with the long review

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.490
of the medical history of abortion.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:07.200
It reviews the history of abortion laws,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.180
finding that many prohibitions on abortion

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.650
were relatively modern.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.880
The Court reviewed many religious

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.170
and philosophical traditions,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.430
which contain many different views

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.270
about when or if the unborn
could be considered people

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.160
during different stages of pregnancy.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.290
And the Court ruled that the word person,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:25.200
as used in the constitution,
did not include the unborn.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.320
The Court concluded that
the 14th Amendment's concept

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.200
of personal liberty includes
a right of personal privacy

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.060
over certain areas, which include

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.880
whether a woman terminates
a pregnancy or not.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.680
And the Court also recognized
that the government

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.510
could not make a choice
about whether the person

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:40.830
continues a pregnancy.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.540
And the Court held that the
state had valid interest

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.070
that justified regulation of pregnancy,

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.570
and that the state's interest increased

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.250
as the pregnancy progressed.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.350
Then almost 20 years later,
the Supreme Court decided

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.570
another abortion case that
substantially revised Roe,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.430
that's Planned Parenthood v. Casey.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.200
That case tested whether
states had the right

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.960
to restrict abortion, including
during the first trimester.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.610
The Pennsylvania regulations included

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:00.870
a mandatory waiting period,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.060
spousal notification,
and parental consent.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.130
In the Casey decision, the
Supreme Court overturned

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:08.130
Roe's trimester rule in favor
of a viability analysis,

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.710
and lowered the standard of
review for abortion restrictions

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.860
from strict scrutiny to
an undue burden test.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.750
And under Casey's undue burden test,

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.060
abortion restrictions
were unconstitutional

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.770
if they had the purpose or effect

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.810
of placing a substantial obstacle

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.760
in the path of a woman seeking an abortion

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.590
of a non-viable fetus.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.900
The Court held that states
could require parental consent

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.240
for a minor's abortion,
require waiting period

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.160
between seeking and obtaining an abortion,

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.500
and require detailed informed consent,

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.840
including medical information
about the abortion.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.220
But the state couldn't require a woman

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.230
to sign a form stating
that she had given notice

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.450
to her husband that she
was having an abortion.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.690
And again, following in
these cases, in 2003,

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.180
the Supreme Court cited
Loving, Griswold, Eisenstadt,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.850
Casey, and Roe in
striking down a Texas law

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.790
that criminalized sexual
intimacy by same sex couples

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.340
but not identical conduct
by straight couples.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:57.750
The Court held that the law violated

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.640
the 14th Amendment's Due Process Clause

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.190
as adults are free to
engage in private conduct

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:03.390
in the exercise of their liberty

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.070
under the Due Process Clause.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.190
Quote, "Their right to liberty
under the Due Process Clause

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.380
gives them the full right
to engage in their conduct

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.300
without intervention of the government."

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.820
And notably, Justice Scalia
dissented in this case,

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.350
stating that the logic of the decision

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.020
would of course permit same-sex marriage.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.810
And in 2012, following in that case,

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.570
the Court proved him right by
legalizing same-sex marriage,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.347
citing the same string
of cases that established

00:06:26.347 --> 00:06:28.650
the right to choose one's
intimate relationships

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:30.750
and whether to have children.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:34.530
And when the Court decided that
case, Obergefell v. Hodges,

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.810
it observed that if a person
has the right to choose

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:38.220
whether to have an abortion,

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.390
then they have the right to marry

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:40.710
the person of their choice.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.960
Now, in the aftermath of
Planned Parenthood v. Casey,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.970
states enacted more and more restrictions

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.590
on the right to an abortion.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:48.510
Over the years, federal
courts have analyzed

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.400
nearly every kind of abortion
restriction imaginable,

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.220
invalidating some of them
and upholding others.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:54.420
But one thing never changed,

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.970
the Court's original ruling
that the right to privacy

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.160
included the right to illegal abortion.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:06:59.993
But the recent case

00:06:59.993 --> 00:07:02.130
of Dobbs v. Jackson
Women's Health Organization

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.240
changed all of that.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.070
The Jackson Women's Health Organization

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:06.540
brought this lawsuit to challenge

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:09.450
the constitutionality of
Mississippi's Gestational Age Act,

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.030
which states, "Except
in a medical emergency

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:14.910
or in the case of a
severe fetal abnormality,

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:18.300
a person shall not intentionally
or knowingly perform

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.090
or induce an abortion
of an unborn human being

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.970
if the probable gestational
age of the unborn human being

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.610
has been determined to be
greater than 15 weeks."

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:27.810
Jackson Women's Health

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.210
was the only remaining
abortion clinic in the state.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.940
The organization won at the
federal district court level,

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:34.590
and the district court decision was upheld

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.120
by the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.130
Mississippi appealed to the Supreme Court.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.320
Now, some legal commentators
expected the Supreme Court

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:42.810
to simply overturn
Casey's viability analysis

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.970
and replace it with a right to an abortion

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:46.800
that stops at 15 weeks.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.900
And in fact, this is what
Justice Roberts advocated

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.400
in his concurring opinion.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.590
However, a majority of the Supreme Court

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:56.310
decided to overrule Roe
and Casey from the roots

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:57.600
and allow each state to decide

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.030
whether there is a right
to an abortion or not.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.400
The majority opinion was
written by Justice Alito,

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.740
and the vote to overturn
Roe was five to four.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.590
Justices Thomas, Gorsuch,
Kavanaugh, and Barrett

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:09.030
joined Alito's opinion.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:11.550
Chief Justice John Roberts
did not join in the opinion

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.130
in overturning Roe but filed
a separate concurring opinion,

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.260
which he agreed with the result,

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:17.790
which is why you'll see
some people call this

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.170
a six to three decision.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:22.230
Justices Kagan, Sotomayor,
and Breyer dissented.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.330
Now, Justice Alito's
opinion begins by stating

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.180
that abortion presents
a profound moral issue

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:30.180
on which Americans hold
sharply conflicting views.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.090
The Constitution's text
does not refer to abortion.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.580
Alito concedes that not
all rights recognized

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:37.200
by the Court have to be mentioned

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.730
in the Constitution's text.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.620
As I explained earlier, the Court has held

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:43.800
that due process guarantees
of the 5th and 14th Amendments

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.720
protect certain substantive rights

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:47.640
that are not listed in the constitution.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.290
The general idea is that certain liberties

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.510
are so important that the
state cannot infringe on them

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:52.650
without a compelling reason,

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:55.320
no matter how much procedural
due process is given.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:57.570
And Justice Alito states
that the Due Process Clause

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.700
protects the right to
interracial marriage,

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:00.990
the right to use contraception,

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:02.910
and the right to same-sex marriage,

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:05.520
but it does not guarantee
the right to abortion.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:06.450
Why?

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.700
Well, according to the majority,

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:11.190
the test for unenumerated
rights is whether the right

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:13.800
is deeply rooted in the
nation's history and tradition

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:16.440
and implicit in the
concept of ordered liberty.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:17.670
And according to Justice Alito,

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.160
abortion doesn't qualify
under that standard.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.360
And in setting forth this test

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.460
for which unenumerated
rights are permissible,

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:25.770
Alito cites Washington v. Glucksberg,

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.260
the case which found that there
was no constitutional right

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.450
to a physician-assisted suicide.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.730
Abortion is like assisted suicide because,

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.410
according to the majority,

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:37.950
neither can trace their roots
to the founding of America.

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.200
The Alito opinion goes on at great length

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.910
about the history of abortion in America.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.610
He states that until the latter
part of the 20th century,

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:47.220
such a right was entirely
unknown in American law.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.230
And indeed, when the 14th
Amendment was adopted,

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:51.150
three quarters of the
states made abortion a crime

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:52.770
at all stages of pregnancy.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.630
And the majority opinion
says that the 50 years

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:56.550
between Roe and the present day

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:58.353
do not carry any weight in establishing

00:09:58.353 --> 00:10:00.210
that that right is deeply rooted

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:03.390
or that women relied on it
in organizing their lives.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.670
The majority opinion
concludes that Roe's holding

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.140
that abortion was a liberty interest

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.150
protected by due process was incorrect.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:11.640
The Roe decision said,
quote, "The right of privacy,

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.890
whether it be founded in
the 14th Amendment's concept

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.950
of personal liberty and
restrictions upon state action,

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.800
as we feel it is, or as the
district court determined

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:21.360
in the Ninth Amendment's preservation

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:22.620
of rights to the people,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.140
is broad enough to
encompass a woman's decision

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.240
whether or not to terminate her pregnancy.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.130
Now, Alito criticized the
justices who wrote this,

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.140
saying that they couldn't
really put their finger

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:34.080
on the nature of the right and
it was decided on feelings.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.180
And then there's always
been controversy about that.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:37.980
In fact, many commentators have argued,

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.330
the Court made a mistake in grounding

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:40.163
the right to an abortion

00:10:40.163 --> 00:10:42.000
in the substance of Due Process Clause.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.100
Justice Ginsburg argued that the Court

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.800
should have decided Roe based
on the Equal Protection Clause

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.930
or the Privileges and Immunities Clause,

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:50.850
which would've provided
a more durable protection

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:51.683
of the right.

00:10:51.683 --> 00:10:54.030
And in Dobbs, several
constitutional law professors

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.940
filed amicus briefs, arguing
that the right to an abortion

00:10:56.940 --> 00:10:59.100
is rooted in the Equal Protection Clause.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.380
But Justice Alito said that
the Equal Protection Clause

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.540
is inapplicable because
the Court had already ruled

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:06.510
that abortion is not a
sex-based classification.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.210
Justice Alito rejected calls
to preserve Roe and Casey

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:10.583
because of stare decisis.

00:11:10.583 --> 00:11:13.080
He said that the concept
is not a straight jacket

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.240
when a ruling is grievously incorrect,

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.130
and listed other landmark decisions

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:19.830
which upended precedent like
Brown v. Board of Education,

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:22.770
which overruled Plessy
v. Ferguson's separate

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:24.447
but equal doctrine.

00:11:24.447 --> 00:11:26.040
And then there was stare decisis,

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.930
the idea that the Court
should follow precedent

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:29.490
to foster predictability

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:31.230
unless it has a very good reason not to.

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.050
And in assessing whether
stare decisis applied,

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:35.490
the Court looked to several factors,

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.050
the nature of the Court's error,

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:38.460
the quality of the reasoning,

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:39.780
the workability of the standard,

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:41.490
it's effect on other parts of the law,

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:42.930
and reliance interests.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.630
Justice Alito said stare
decisis was not applicable

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:48.120
because the original Roe
decision, as modified by Casey,

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.400
is, quote, "Egregiously wrong."

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:52.860
In support of its decision,
the majority opinion

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:54.240
cites 30 other cases where the Court

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:56.040
has overturned prior precedent.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.710
As to the five factors, Justice Alito said

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.390
that Roe was fundamentally unfair

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:01.860
because it foreclosed the opportunity

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:03.180
for anti-abortion advocates

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:04.950
to lobby their government for change.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:06.300
Quote, "Those on the losing side,

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.100
those who sought to advance
the state's interest

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:09.900
in fetal life could no longer seek

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.640
to persuade their elected representatives

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:14.280
to adopt policies consistent
with their views,"

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:15.840
though the statement ignores the decades

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:17.580
of law-making around abortion,

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:19.770
where legislatures passed
abortion restrictions

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:21.870
in response to the democratic process.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:24.270
And as to the workability factor,

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.040
Alito said that as a practical matter,

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.130
the undue burden test was
too confusing to apply.

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:32.160
Though he also criticized Roe's
original trimester framework

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:34.140
as being plucked out of nowhere.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:36.090
And in a stunningly short section,

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.130
Alito wrote that overturning the precedent

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:40.830
would not hurt women
because there's no evidence

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:42.510
that women rely on the right to abortion

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:43.710
in living their lives.

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:46.290
He said, quote, "Conventional
concrete reliance interests

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:47.310
are not present here."

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:49.500
And he concluded that
the right to an abortion

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.300
had nothing in common with
other types of interest

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.120
that people rely on, like
contract and property rights,

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.430
because abortion is unplanned.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.020
And he said that if women
really think they rely

00:12:58.020 --> 00:12:59.730
on the right to abortion,
they should vote.

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:02.070
He dismissed generalized
issues of reliance

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:04.020
by saying the Court is ill-equipped

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:07.620
to assess generalized assertions
about the national psyche.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.990
Now, obviously, four other
justices, Roberts, Sotomayor,

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.730
Kagan, and Breyer, criticized the majority

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:14.490
for overruling two
longstanding precedents.

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:15.990
Justice Roberts' concurring opinion,

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:17.550
which we'll talk about more in a second,

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.130
supported upholding Roe
because of stare decisis.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.020
And meanwhile, the dissenters disagreed

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.000
with the majority's
conclusion that it can reverse

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.860
these abortion cases without undermining

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.420
other fundamental rights.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:29.520
They wrote, quote, "Think
of someone telling you

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.220
that the Jenga tower
simply will not collapse."

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:34.020
However, Justice Alito
addressed their concerns

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:36.900
by stating that the right
to same sex intimate acts,

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:37.800
the right to contraception,

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:39.600
and the right to same-sex marriage

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:42.900
were quantifiably different
than the right to an abortion.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:45.780
Unlike those rights, abortion
terminates a human life.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.640
It affects, quote, potential life.

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:50.070
Whereas, those other rights do not.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:51.960
He said, quote, "Nothing in this opinion

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:53.190
should be understood to cast doubt

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:55.560
on precedents that do
not concern abortion."

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.480
Though it should be noted
that this distinction

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:00.180
between rights that
implicate potential life

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:01.620
is not in the constitution

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:05.040
and is not particularly well
supported in this context.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:06.450
Justice Alito goes on to say

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:09.300
that since abortion doesn't
implicate any fundamental right,

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:11.250
then Mississippi's law
should not be judged

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:12.510
according to the heightened scrutiny

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:14.640
that the Court affords fundamental rights.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.080
Instead, the majority opinion says

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:17.850
that abortion restrictions
should be judged

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.400
using the rational basis
standard of review,

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.770
which is the most lenient
standard of review.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:27.000
Basically, almost no law
ever fails rational basis.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:28.770
Now, it's relatively easy for state laws

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:30.240
to survive rational basis scrutiny.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:33.570
The laws are upheld as long
as there is a rational basis,

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:35.580
which the legislature could have thought

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:38.880
it would serve the
legitimate state interest.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.310
And Alito had no problem
finding that Mississippi statute

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:43.050
that met that standard since it asserted

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:45.810
the state's interest in
protecting the life of the unborn,

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:47.910
and because it regulated
the medical procedure

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.940
that is generally used for
abortions after the 15th week.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:52.800
And therefore, the opinion concluded

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:55.590
the abortion providers'
challenge to the law had to fail.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:57.690
And the Alito opinion did not
analyze a women's interest

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:00.180
in obtaining an abortion
after 15 weeks of pregnancy

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:02.070
because under the rational basis review,

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.870
the Court outlined it wasn't necessary

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:05.640
to the determination of this case.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.100
And that takes us to
the Roberts concurrence.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.590
Now, the concurrences and the dissents

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.090
do not carry the force of law.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:15.150
There was a majority for the
Court and that is now the law.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:18.720
But examining these other
concurrences and dissents

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:20.910
is interesting to let
us know where the court

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:22.530
might be going in the future

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:24.570
and what the other alternatives were.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:26.550
With respect to the Roberts concurrence,

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:28.920
Chief Justice Roberts
agreed with the decision

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:30.450
to uphold the Mississippi law,

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:31.290
but he would've done so

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.780
without formally
overturning Roe and Casey.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:35.370
Roberts was prepared to uphold

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:37.080
the constitutional right to an abortion,

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.780
but he would've eliminated
the viability analysis

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:41.700
that was in place in Casey.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:43.410
He would've characterized Roe and Casey

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:46.350
as adopting two distinct
rules of constitutional law,

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:47.850
a woman's right to end a pregnancy

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:49.920
and the state's power to
override a woman's right

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:51.750
when a fetus becomes viable.

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:54.030
Justice Roberts said
that the viability line,

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:55.860
quote, "Never made any sense."

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:57.030
The right to terminate a pregnancy

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:58.530
should, quote, "extend far enough

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:01.110
to ensure a reasonable
opportunity to choose

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:03.240
but need not extend any further."

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:05.280
Roberts briefly surveys international law

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:06.810
and concludes that the general proposition

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:09.630
is to allow abortions
until around 12 to 15 weeks

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:11.850
without regard to the
viability of the fetus.

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:13.440
Roberts also points out that Mississippi

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.560
did not ask the Court to eliminate
the right to an abortion.

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.420
The basic request was straightforward.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:19.830
He wrote that it was to clarify

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:22.590
whether abortion
prohibitions before viability

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.600
are always unconstitutional.

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.460
For Roberts, all of
these factors indicated

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:27.870
that the best way to balance the interest

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:29.700
was to use judicial restraint.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:31.830
He would've eliminated
the viability standard

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:33.840
but upheld the right to an abortion.

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:35.940
Justice Roberts first
suggested this compromise

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:37.140
during oral arguments,

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:39.780
but he was unable to sway
the five other justices.

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:40.950
Then there are the concurrences

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:42.870
from Justices Kavanaugh and Thomas.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:44.910
Justice Brett Kavanaugh
said that in his view,

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:46.920
the constitution is neutral on abortion.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:48.360
Since the constitution is neutral,

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:49.620
then, quote, "This Court

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:52.080
must also be scrupulously
neutral about abortion."

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:53.670
Kavanaugh emphasized that the Court

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:55.290
wasn't outlawing abortion nationwide

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:57.870
and was instead leaving
the decision to the states.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:00.540
He wrote that the nine
unelected members of this Court

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:02.040
do not possess the
constitutional authority

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:03.630
to override a democratic process

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:07.170
and to decree either pro-life
or pro-choice abortion policy

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.720
for all 330 people living
in the United States.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.670
The rest of Kavanaugh's
opinion was designed

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:14.010
to reassure people that this
wasn't a radical opinion

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:17.310
that would upend other cases
like Griswold and Obergefell.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:19.770
And he anticipates some
of the major legal issues

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:21.120
that are probably coming,

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:23.220
particularly whether a
state can ban its residents

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:26.280
from traveling to another
state to obtain an abortion,

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:28.890
or whether a state can
retroactively punish people

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.900
who had abortions.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.790
He says that these are not hard questions.

00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:34.350
The answer to both of them is no.

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:35.520
Though this is just a concurrence,

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:38.130
these answers in this
concurrence have no legal weight

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:39.690
since these questions
were before the Court

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:42.450
and no other justices
agreed with his conclusions.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.220
So that takes us to Thomas' concurrence,

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:47.610
which is sort of the opposite
of Kavanaugh's concurrence.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.800
Justice Thomas wrote
a concurrence to state

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:51.420
that while he agreed with
the majority opinion,

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.150
that there was never a due
process right to abortion.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:55.470
The Court should also make it clear

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:56.550
that there was no such thing

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:58.650
as substantive due process at all.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:17:59.490
Justice Thomas believes

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:00.810
that the Constitution's Due Process Clause

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:03.210
only addresses the processes
the government uses

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:05.250
before taking a person's
life, liberty, or property,

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:07.650
that it has nothing to
say about the substance

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:08.520
of the person's rights.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:09.990
He left no doubt about what he thought

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:11.730
about rights to gay
marriage, contraception,

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:13.320
and same-sex intimate conduct.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.900
Quote, "In future cases,
we should reconsider

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:17.940
all of the Court's substantive
due process precedents,

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:19.890
including Griswold,
Lawrence, and Obergefell.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:21.750
Though critics noted that
Thomas did not address

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:23.640
the similar case of Loving v. Virginia,

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:25.950
the case that legalized
interracial marriage

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:29.040
and protects the legitimacy
of Thomas' own marriage.

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.440
It's hard to square a
deeply rooted analysis

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:33.720
with keeping Loving v. Virginia intact

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:35.310
if the Court is going to go after the rest

00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:37.230
of the unenumerated rights cases.

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:40.320
However, Loving was also
grounded in equal protection.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:42.510
It is related to but not totally dependent

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:45.660
on these substantive due process
cases he wants to overturn.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:48.360
That takes us to the
dissent of Justices Breyer,

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:50.550
Sotomayor, and Kagan.

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:52.500
The dissenters critiqued
the majority's claim

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:55.020
that they were simply returning the issue

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:56.640
to state legislatures where it belongs.

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:58.320
Quote, "We believe in a constitution

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:01.080
that puts some issues off
limits to majority rule."

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:03.750
In other words, the
dissenting opinion warns

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:05.220
that it is dangerous for the majority

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:07.500
to have total control
over minority rights.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.930
The dissent rejects Alito's
claim that Roe and Casey

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.780
just randomly appeared,
that they came from nowhere,

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:13.867
and went nowhere.

00:19:13.867 --> 00:19:15.390
"This," they write, "is not true."

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:18.120
The dissenting opinion takes
issue with Alito's statement

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:19.560
that Casey and Roe didn't allow courts

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:20.850
to even consider whether the state

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:22.140
has an interest in promoting life.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:23.580
And the dissent says
that part of the reason

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:26.100
that Casey had departed from
the Roe trimester analysis

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:29.070
was because the Court recognized
that after two decades,

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:31.500
the Roe framework did not
give states sufficient ability

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:33.840
to regulate abortion prior to viability.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:35.640
Quote, "The strength of
those state interests

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:37.830
is exactly why the Court
allowed greater restrictions

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:40.110
on the abortion right than on other rights

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:41.670
derived from the 14th amendment."

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:43.357
The dissent goes on to say that, quote,

00:19:43.357 --> 00:19:44.970
"What Roe and Casey also recognized,

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:46.170
which today's majority does not,

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:47.940
is that a woman's freedom and equality

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:48.810
are likewise involved.

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:51.390
That fact, the presence of
countervailing interest,

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:53.340
is what made the abortion question hard

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:54.990
and what necessitated balancing.

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:56.490
The majority scoffs at that idea,

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.310
castigating us for repeatedly
praising the balance

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:00.480
the two cases arrived at,

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:02.010
with the word balance in scare quotes.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.260
To the majority, balance is a dirty word,

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:07.200
as moderation is a foreign concept."

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:09.900
The dissenters also disagreed
with Alito's insistence

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:11.880
that the right to an abortion
must be deeply rooted

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:13.320
in the country's history.

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:15.270
The Court, quote, "Makes this change

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:18.210
based on a single question,
did the reproductive right

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:21.450
recognized in Roe and Casey exist in 1868,

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:23.430
the year when the 14th
amendment was ratified.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:25.530
The majority says, and
with this much we agree,

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.240
that the answer to this question is no.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:30.150
In 1868, there was no nationwide right

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.130
to end a pregnancy and no thought

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:33.480
that the 14th Amendment provided one."

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:35.280
But the dissent concludes
that it doesn't make sense

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.080
to ask what was in the minds of the people

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:39.150
who ratified the amendment in 1868,

00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:40.237
and more to the point, quote,

00:20:40.237 --> 00:20:42.450
"People did not ratify the 14th Amendment.

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:43.283
Men did.

00:20:43.283 --> 00:20:45.510
So it is perhaps not
surprising that the ratifiers

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:47.580
were not perfectly
attuned to the importance

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:49.380
of reproductive rights for women's liberty

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:51.300
or for their capacity to participate

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:53.250
as equal members in our nation.

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.020
The dissent notes that Alito still thought

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:57.360
it was necessary to trace
hostility to abortion,

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:00.690
and of course, the women's
rights back to the 1300s.

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.090
Justice Alito relied
heavily on the writings

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:05.040
of people like Matthew
Hale, a British jurist

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:07.740
who presided at early
American witch trials

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:10.470
and believed women were
property of their husbands.

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:12.540
And the dissent doesn't
see why Hale's writings

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:15.180
or any thoughts by people
before the Revolutionary War

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:17.160
should play a role in
what is acceptable today.

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:19.920
Quote, "First, it is
not clear what relevance

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:22.710
such early history should
have even to the majority."

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:25.350
And then the Court cites
the Bruen gun rights case,

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:26.460
which was decided last week,

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:29.400
where the Court held that,
quote, "Historical evidence

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:32.070
that long predates
ratification may not illuminate

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:33.390
the scope of the right."

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:36.360
And the last bit is a dig at
the majority opinion in Bruen,

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.120
where the same justices
who overruled Roe and Casey

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.160
concluded that when it came to guns,

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:43.080
it didn't actually matter
if the people were allowed

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:45.810
to carry concealed firearms
prior to ratification

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:46.643
of the Second Amendment.

00:21:46.643 --> 00:21:48.030
And the dissent accuses the majority

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:49.410
of picking and choosing the history

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:50.760
that supports the outcome they want.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:53.100
Quote, "If early history
obviously supported

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:55.110
abortion rights, the
majority would no doubt say

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:57.660
that only the views of the
14th Amendment's ratifiers

00:21:57.660 --> 00:21:58.493
were germane."

00:21:58.493 --> 00:22:00.060
And many people have accused Alito

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:02.880
of this sort of Goldilocks
quasi historical analysis,

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:05.760
that the rights can't be too old

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:08.250
and they also can't be too new.

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:11.220
Roe has been the law for 50
years and that does not qualify,

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:13.740
according to Alito, as
being deeply rooted.

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:16.800
And the leaked draft attracted
attention from historians

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:19.230
who said that Alito got the
early history of abortion wrong.

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:21.900
Many states Alito listed
as banning all abortion

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:23.640
actually banned it only after quickening

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:26.820
and did not make abortion criminal at all.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:28.080
The same historical evidence made it

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:30.450
into the official majority opinion.

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:32.850
But the dissenters took note
of this controversy, saying,

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:35.040
quote, "Embarrassingly for the majority,

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:37.530
early law in fact does
provide some support

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:38.850
for abortion rights.

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:40.620
Common-law authorities
did not treat abortion

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.300
as a crime before quickening,

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:44.460
the point when the fetus
has moved in the womb.

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:47.190
And early American law
followed the common-law rule.

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:49.350
So the criminal law of that early time

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:50.910
might be taken as roughly consonant

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:53.220
with Roe's and Casey's different treatment

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:54.870
of early and late abortions."

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:57.180
And this is often a
controversy when you're talking

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:58.140
about originalism.

00:22:58.140 --> 00:22:59.370
In some sense, both the majority

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:00.780
and the dissent are correct.

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:03.000
At many times in American
history but not all,

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:04.710
states criminalized abortions.

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:06.120
And at many times in American history,

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:08.970
abortion was permitted, and
not just in the 20th century.

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:10.380
This just shows how difficult it is

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:11.730
to use historical evidence

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:14.520
to find the right
interpretation of the text.

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:16.350
And since early American law allowed

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:17.820
for legal abortion before quickening,

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:20.250
the dissent says Alito
focuses on modern law

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:21.900
where he concludes that
because most states

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:25.200
criminalized abortion in the
decades before Roe was decided,

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:26.670
the right wasn't deeply rooted.

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:28.710
And again, the dissent
points out the majority

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:32.040
for the perceived hypocrisy,
given the Bruen decision.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:35.520
Quote, "Had the pre-Roe
liberalization of abortion laws

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:38.400
occurred more quickly and more
widely in the 20th century,

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:40.080
the majority would say once again

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:42.750
that only the ratifiers
views are germane."

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:44.700
And the dissent ends by accusing Alito

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:46.980
of erasing women and their
interest from its opinion.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:49.530
Quote, "The majority would
allow states to ban abortion

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:52.140
from conception onwards
because it does not think

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.390
forced childbirth at all
implicates women's rights

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:55.800
to equality and freedom.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:58.110
Today's Court, that is, does
not think there is anything

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:00.150
of constitutional significance attached

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:03.420
to a woman's control of her
body and the path of her life.

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:06.630
Roe and Casey thought that
one-sided view misguided.

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:08.970
In some sense, that is a
difference in a nutshell

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:11.370
between our precedence
and the majority opinion.

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:13.230
The constitutional regime we have lived in

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:15.510
for the last 50 years
recognized competing interests,

00:24:15.510 --> 00:24:17.340
and sought to balance between them.

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:19.050
The constitutional regime we enter today

00:24:19.050 --> 00:24:21.690
erases the woman's interest and recognizes

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:23.790
only the state's or the
Federal Government's."

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:25.020
Now, the dissent tears

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:27.120
into the majority's
stare decisis analysis,

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:29.880
noting that, quote, "The Court
may not overrule a decision,

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:32.640
even a constitutional one,
without a special justification."

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:34.020
And as to the other factors,

00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:35.460
whether the standard is workable,

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:37.410
whether there have been
changes in the law or fact,

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:39.510
and whether women relied
on the right to abortion,

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:41.250
the dissent takes Alito to task.

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:43.200
Quote, "The standards
Roe and Casey set out

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:44.100
are perfectly workable.

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:45.750
No changes in either law or fact

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:47.190
have eroded the two decisions.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:49.350
And tens of millions of American women

00:24:49.350 --> 00:24:52.170
have relied and continued to
rely on the right to abortion.

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:54.720
So under traditional
stare decisis principles,

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:56.760
the majority has no special justification

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:58.320
for the harm it causes."

00:24:58.320 --> 00:24:59.587
And according to the dissent, quote,

00:24:59.587 --> 00:25:01.650
"The majority comes close
to conceding that point.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:03.270
The majority barely mentions any legal

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:05.970
or factual changes that have
occurred since Roe and Casey.

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:07.920
It suggests the two
decisions are hard for courts

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:09.840
to implement, but cannot prove its case.

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:11.640
In the end, the majority
says, all it must say

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:13.950
to override stare decisis is one thing,

00:25:13.950 --> 00:25:16.860
that it believes Roe and
Casey egregiously wrong.

00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:19.260
That rule could equally spell
the end of any precedent

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:22.260
with which a bare majority of
the present court disagrees."

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:24.330
And the dissent makes
a legal realism point

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:25.260
incredibly bluntly.

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:27.750
Quote, "The majority has
overruled Roe and Casey

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:29.520
for one and only one reason,

00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:31.050
because it has always despised them,

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:33.150
and now it has the votes to discard them.

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:35.280
The majority thereby
substitutes a rule by judges

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:36.300
for the rule of law."

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:38.490
Now, I know a lot of people
are upset about this decision,

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:41.760
and we'll talk more about what
can be done in the future.

